From efcae0a1d0c668e4985fbf76f22027b9c498ad49 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(runtime): share native actor animation and resource
 playback

Provide the shared float Molang, actor graph, geometry, accepted resource, sound and particle runtime before account appearance management. Keep native event timing, resource limits and connection lifetimes together so server actors and equipped models can consume the same implementation without Character Creator account access.

Retain the existing renderer and account behavior in their following feature patch.

Own the native physical-bounds payload in the shared actor bridge. Camera
targeting and UI actor views both need dimensions with the actor lifetime,
so neither consumer defines or registers a second bounds transport.

Consume the core block_identifiers_v2 mirror with both native layers and state properties. Expose exact per-layer state lookup for native world queries while particle expiration continues to use primary identities. Bind payload reception to the captured connection and invalidate queued work on disconnect so an old world cannot populate a new session.

The particle block-filter integration test now also verifies that an extra-layer update cannot change a primary-layer expiration result. This prepares the world source for native aim queries; it does not claim completed aim target selection or rendering.

Apply the retained native actor scale during living render-state extraction.
CubeCraft initially sends four local-skin copies at scale 0.0001, then
uses scale one for visible previews and zero for hidden actors. The Java
render state kept scale one after those metadata updates. Replace its
scale before shadow extraction, so the body, equipment and shadow share
the native value without the Java attribute clamp. Actors without a
native snapshot retain the ordinary Java scale.

Use the target 1.26.51.1 player body control for remote player actors.
Player body control differs from the generic mob routine: retain the
0.05-block horizontal movement threshold, 0.3 turn smoothing, 75-degree
outer limit and 0.75 correction toward the 50-degree soft limit. Riding
players bypass the relative-yaw clamp. Normalize with native float
wrapping. The Java backward-walk target and hard clamp otherwise leave
standing server NPCs at a different body angle after rotation updates.

Native evidence: Windows build 51061372, protocol 2193, executable SHA256
537c0aee2e79afbdc94b44b28e00f466ae62bc50e2733d953b430db9dbaa9ee7.
Player constructor virtual slot 0x558 selects 1401e1dd0, whose control
vtable selects 14387a200. BodyControlSystem callback 144893380 invokes
that player callback and normalizes the current/previous rotations.
Compare 2,268 direct and 320 chained original native system callbacks
using the official UCRT atan2f/fmodf. All Java results match bit for bit.
The native PassengerComponent lookup supplies the riding branch.

Bind only remote Java players with native actor state. Preserve float
position subtraction and use the existing translated swing state. These
checks establish the body calculation and client binding, not full
network interpolation, swing timing or visible native scene parity.

Read every weighted alias in an animation-reference object. The licensed
1.26.51.1 camel controller groups adult and baby animation aliases in a
single object; rejecting objects with multiple members prevents its
whole graph from loading. Apply the same reader to controller states
and root animation entries, retaining declaration order and the existing
aggregate reference bound. The grouped-reference regression covers both
paths and conditional adult/baby weights without copied native assets.
This does not establish native JSON object evaluation order for
side-effecting weights.

Replace repeated animation definitions in their admitted file order and
retain that order when grouping bundled files. CubeCraft's protocol 2193
lobby includes two different animation.cubecraft.logo.idle definitions;
rejecting the catalog prevented its unrelated player graph from loading.
Retain the other resource catalogs' existing conflict policy.

The original Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 animation registration helper
141bf7070 replaces the existing data through native lookup 141bba610 and
destroys the old definition. Nine executable registrations verify six
replacements and retention of unrelated keys. The fixture supplies group
metadata, allocation/free, single-thread mutex boundaries, CFG, finite CRT
helpers and the default Molang compilation seam. Native file enumeration
and full animation-schema decoding remain outside this fixture. Native
lowercase name construction was observed; general reference normalization
remains open.

Behavioral tests sample replacement poses in both admission orders and
through higher-pack overrides, without retaining old bones or losing
unrelated animations. Captured assets and native probes remain private.

Apply native ASCII folding when registering animation definition names in
bundled, accepted-server and direct JSON graph paths. Keep references
unchanged: native public group lookup hashes their exact spelling.
Canonicalize before replacement so differently cased registrations retain
the last definition instead of creating independent, unreachable entries.

The target 1.26.51.1 converter 1403201d0 matches ASCII folding in 514 byte
and mixed-Unicode cases, including heap-sized strings. Original group
lookup 141bbb210 resolves the lowercase registration and leaves differently
cased references without data. These four controls execute alongside the
original registration and hash-map helpers. Manager metadata, allocation,
uncontended mutexes, CFG, finite CRT helpers and default Molang compilation
remain fixture boundaries; file decoding and rendering are not claimed.

Pose tests cover bundled and accepted libraries and direct JSON graphs,
including case-colliding complete definitions, preserved non-ASCII
characters, unchanged reference spelling and unrelated animation retention.

Allow ordinary pack-relative particle atlas paths instead of requiring the
textures/ folder. Galaxite's captured gem_bag_particle selects font/glyph_E1,
whose PNG is already retained in the shared native resource archive. Remove
the emitter parser and resource lookup folder guards together; keep the
existing archive path validation and image bounds.

The target Bedrock 1.26.51.1/protocol 2193 particle reader 142152f40 reads
and copies the texture field without adding or requiring a folder prefix.
An instruction probe executes 142153906..142153a4b with native JSON field
and string helpers: nine string cases, including font/custom/root names,
retain their values; three non-string cases fail the type check. This
probe enters after material dispatch and excludes complete schema loading,
subsequent resource lookup and rendering. CRT memory/string operations,
allocation and diagnostic backends are host boundaries. Do not infer
acceptance by later resource stages from the field reader alone.

Tests exercise generated PNGs outside textures/ through the native actor
archive, pack reconstruction, particle decoding and emitter output. A
29-connection resource audit finds the real Galaxite font atlas and five
invisible underscore placeholders outside the conventional texture folder.

Read client-world, direct chunk, vanilla render-region and optional Sodium
fluid queries from the authoritative native layers. Use the core's forward
liquid-state mapping rather than trying to waterlog Java carriers. Preserve
mapped water/lava level and falling properties; unknown definitions and
ordinary Java worlds retain vanilla queries. Render workers read the
thread-safe mirror without invoking Minecraft lifecycle mutation.

The target 1.26.51.1 native selector at 142d80600 chooses a non-air extra
layer before primary, verified across 180 pinned-binary cases with real
layer getters and supplied chunk-provider/palette boundaries.

The mesh compilers retain the selected native fluid beside each primary
block and skip a cell only when both its primary geometry and fluid are
empty. Forward native neighbor fluids through face and height reads,
including Sodium's hidden-fluid exposure walk, without replacing the
primary blocks used for shape and occlusion. Preserve vanilla fallback
when the native view is unavailable or a definition is unknown. Query
primitive cell coordinates directly, avoiding per-cell and extra neighbor
position allocations in the chunk builders.

This integrates authoritative layer inputs with the existing mesh
algorithms. Native face occlusion, vertex-height parity and complete
physical simulation remain unverified; the selector probe does not
establish those behaviors.

Invalidate affected mesh sections when native fluid selection changes,
even if the primary Java carrier stays identical. Include a two-block horizontal
and one-block vertical halo for face/height samples and Sodium 0.9.2's
five-by-five hidden-fluid exposure walk. Full snapshots and chunk
removal invalidate their regions; new Java chunks retain their normal
packet-driven rebuild. Scope the native update tracker to each reset
and clear it with the view on level/connection changes. Bound section
coordinates before calling the client's render invalidation API.

Tests cover wet/dry transitions, equivalent aliases, unchanged fluid,
negative boundary coordinates, dimension resets, forget/clear and integer
limits. This makes layer updates visible without depending on an unrelated
Java block change to trigger a rebuild.

Share the existing inherited-basis reset with equipped model bindings.
Native composition 0x141bc3d20 retains the translated origin and clears
the basis for entity-relative rotation after accepting a copied matrix.
Keep the common operation in the actor transform helper so explicit
bindings and ordinary bones use the same rule. Ordinary composition
retains its position, rotation and scale order.

Start each emitter with a fresh unbound query context before lifetime
configuration and the first creation expression. Animation pre-effect
variables survive, but captured actor queries and animation clocks do
not. Detach missing-query tracking from the animation owner so an unbound
creation expression cannot invalidate the owner's rendering context.
Retain refreshed actor/count queries on loop restarts and reused children.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1/build51061372/protocol2193, binary SHA-256
537c0aee2e79afbdc94b44b28e00f466ae62bc50e2733d953b430db9dbaa9ee7:
original factory 14216ba90 through first creation dispatch in 142157210
initializes actor and count-owner pointers to zero even with a dirty
allocation. Eleven original scalar/count callbacks return zero against
that captured context. Allocation, bounded CRT copies, mutex operations,
resource lookup and the component callback are supplied boundaries.
Random-variable registration is explicitly skipped. This proves the
constructor query boundary, not JSON/Molang evaluation, actor admission,
child lifetime parity or visible native rendering.

Refresh common particle actor queries when sampling attachments, rather
than retaining the last actor draw's motion, water state and metadata.
Server particle packets now share native property, variant and spell-color
inputs with animation-bound effects. Keep each sampled map immutable and
preserve unrelated graph inputs and emitter/particle variables. Retire
server bindings when their captured native actor lifetime changes.

Eight pinned 1.26.51.1 controls run original initialization/context refresh
1421603e0/142159bd0 once, then original speed and water callbacks
14220ecd0/14220f040 after in-place ECS and motion changes on the same actor.
Actor resolution, level/physical-component getters, variable access and
expression callbacks are supplied. No later context refresh, native actor
animation, property evaluation, live server or GPU runs in these controls.
This supports fresh actor reads, not complete native query producer parity.

Model manual child handles for their owning emitter's full lifetime. An
expired registered child still receives initialization, while a retired
handle stays initialized and does not create a replacement. Failed initial
creation remains retryable. Weak references prevent cached retired handles
from retaining entire child runtimes. Parent retirement expires its valid
manual children without deleting their particles or adopting another
emitter that occupies a retired registry slot.

Remove the manual-rate admission check from child construction. Ordinary
rate components have zero manual capacity but still own valid emitters and
can emit automatically on later updates. Preserve native expiry callbacks
before closing emission, and remove parent counts/registry membership before
its child retirement callbacks. Full-manager shutdown keeps its separate
clear path.

Forty-six pinned 1.26.51.1 controls execute leaf dispatcher 142164cb0,
resource lookup 142158740, force expiry 14215cc60/142158940, manual emission
142160c20, and literal/zero manual-capacity getters 1462a4d10/140076c20.
Parent destructor 142170320 runs from entry through child retirement,
stopping before cache/resource cleanup at 1421703c2. Cache entries, handles,
components and emitter memory are supplied. Factory returns, initialization,
context refresh, birth allocation and source expressions remain boundaries.
An expiry callback tail-calls original emission to observe flag ordering.
Native instant and steady rate tables use the zero manual-capacity getter.
The controls exclude native JSON/Molang evaluation, actual particle
allocation, complete object destruction, live server behavior and pixels.

Preserve native manual-child cache traversal for retirement callbacks.
Use the effect name's FNV-1 hash, eight initial buckets, native collision
placement and growth/rebucketing order. Failed factory attempts reserve a
cache entry before construction and retain that entry for retry, including
unavailable child definitions. Rollback clears initialization without
removing its ordering slot. Keep weak successful/stale handle semantics.

Thirty-two pinned native controls execute full leaf dispatcher 142164cb0
from an empty cache, including insertion, collisions and rehashes. They
record 2,870 insertion/retry receipts and run parent destructor 142170320
through its child loop, stopping before cleanup at 1421703c2. Inputs include
parsed names/hashes, empty cache layout, handles and child records. Allocator,
bounded exact CRT copies/comparisons, integer ceilf, free, failed factory
returns and expiry callbacks are supplied boundaries. Cases include real
FNV-1 names, artificial full-hash/bucket collisions, retries and growth
through 513 entries. Native name parsing, resource construction, complete
destruction, reentrant manager allocation, live server and GPU remain open.

Preserve native particle rate composition instead of requiring exactly one
rate. Retain resource order, sum automatic requests against shared capacity
and cached rate state, and initialize capacity from manual getters before
creation expressions. Count manual births so instant rates observe them.
An effect without rates still runs its lifetime and event graph.

128 controls for pinned 1.26.51.1/build51061372/protocol2193 execute complete
factory 14216ba90, original reset 142157210, automatic update 142158a40
through births, and full manual emission 142160c20. The controls compare
1,152 emission steps across all permutations of zero through three rates,
capacity limits, zero capacities and manual/automatic action order.
Resource/material/statistics lookups, manager capacity reservation,
allocation, bounded copies, mutex operations, query refresh, actor absence,
birth allocation, floorf and fmodf are supplied boundaries. Reset skips its
random-variable registration segment. Parsed component arrays and literal
expressions are inputs. Native JSON admission, expression VM execution,
particle aging/motion, complete rendering and pixels remain unverified.
The target-version schema lists the rate components independently.
Four deliberate variants fail these native population comparisons.

Share immutable live actor query snapshots between animation inputs and
particle attachments. Refresh native status flags and metadata without
requiring another actor render, and sample current living-entity state
for local prediction and translated gameplay. Native custom actors can
use non-living Java carriers, so retain their authoritative native flags
instead of replacing unavailable Java status methods with false.

Keep generic particle animation-clock defaults separate from actor data.
Remove the duplicate server-particle status builder and preserve native
float32 motion calculations and emitter-local variables.

Negotiate native query metadata without changing the two existing actor
payload formats. Share has_target between animation and particle scopes;
its native definition tests typed LONG metadata slot 6 against zero and
does not resolve the target actor. Retire a cached query when an older
snapshot cannot supply it instead of silently returning false. Target
1.26.51.1 callback 1422130b0 matches 80 bounded native controls; the core
actor transport patch records their supplied boundaries.

Track unsupported queries per actor frame. A missing input must not poison
later frames after native metadata or conditional branches change. Keep
animation variables, nested query reporting, playback time and effect
cursors intact while starting a fresh admission-error set for each frame.
The regression restores a missing bone query in the same playback and
checks its unchanged animation phase.

Negotiate actor_state_v5 for immutable typed INT metadata. Share verified
skin_id and trade_tier bindings with core controller evaluation and remove
stale values when a legacy snapshot cannot supply them. Keep known native
zero defaults distinct from unavailable metadata. The same actor queries
serve world models, players, custom actors, attachables and bound effects.
The core transport patch records the pinned target callbacks and bounded
execution evidence; do not substitute Java profession or trading levels.

Bind render-controller texture arrays as sampler slots of one draw, not
independent geometry layers. The supported native ENTITY shader families
sample slot zero; extra entries previously duplicated costumes and could
incorrectly mark a secondary Steve/Alex texture as the visible body.
Continue evaluating every texture expression in order so actor variables
and later controllers keep their effects. Separate controllers still draw
separately. Unsupported multi-sampler material families remain rejected.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1's bundled villager_v2 masked controller binds biome and
profession to slots zero and one. Its ordinary AlphaTest sampled fragment
has only the slot-zero resource handle. The extracted target Entity
material SHA-256 is 403efcc7777c9712176f6715f861c873e31d8e45d0b73b8e320bd29754789058.
Regression controls cover independent views, secondary body textures,
ordered expression effects, and separate controller draws. Both controls
fail against the former selector and pass with the corrected binding.
